1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Not everything we go through is easy to understand.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
There are feelings that overwhelm us.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Between sudden calm and endless thoughts.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Between bursts of energy and unexplained exhaustion.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
There is a space we don't fully understand.
"Behind the Silence" is a podcast about that space.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The place where we wonder:
Are we okay? Are we normal?
Or do we need to understand ourselves better?

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
A podcast that brings you closer to yourself,
through real stories, familiar emotions,
and experiences you may have lived before.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:48,000
We don't offer ready-made answers.
We talk, we understand,
and we try to find our way together.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Today, we're exploring a story,
not only about a disorder,
but about a long journey through doubt, fear, and understanding.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Nihal was an ordinary woman,
or at least that's what she believed,
until she felt something changing inside her.
Something bigger than stress or exhaustion.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
A strange, unpredictable feeling
that made her ask every day:
"What's wrong with me?"

12
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:32,000
In this episode, we'll walk with her
from the first moment of doubt,
to confrontation, and finally understanding.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
When did you first feel
that something was different?

14
00:01:36,000 --> 00:02:14,000
I was diagnosed with bipolar disorder in 2017,
but my first episode happened in 2008,
after my father passed away.
I was deeply attached to him.
My mother didn't understand what was happening to me.
People thought I was simply grieving.

15
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,000
What made you realize
it was more than stress or sadness?

16
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:41,000
It was much bigger than that.
I felt like I wasn't myself.
Later, I learned that the manic episodes I had
were not normal.
I barely remember them,
but my family told me about them.

17
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:51,000
If your first episode was in 2008,
why weren't you diagnosed then?

18
00:02:51,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Mental health awareness wasn't what it is today.
I was hospitalized,
and doctors didn't understand my condition.
I was treated as if I were insane.
Imagine being sick
and having everyone think you're crazy.
For years I went through medications
and electroconvulsive therapy.

19
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
What happened after 2013?

20
00:03:34,000 --> 00:04:05,000
I had many episodes.
Sometimes I believed I was the Virgin Mary,
or even the First Lady.
Eventually my sister took me to a doctor
who finally explained my condition.
For the first time,
I realized I wasn't crazy.
I could get treatment and improve.

21
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Then I got married in 2018.
How did marriage affect your mental health?

22
00:04:10,000 --> 00:05:24,000
The first year was good,
but things changed dramatically.
I thought I had recovered.
Living with my husband's family was difficult.
I hoped my mother-in-law would be like a second mother,
but her words often hurt me.
Nothing I did was ever enough.
Later I discovered my husband was cheating on me.
Whenever I confronted him,
he would insult me and damage my self-confidence.
I cried constantly and tried to fix things,
but I never felt seen or appreciated.

23
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:50,000
I endured everything for my children.
Even during arguments,
I pretended everything was fine
because I wanted to protect them.

24
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:20,000
My children are my greatest source of fear and hope.
They're the reason I keep going.
Sometimes I worry they might inherit this disorder.
If that happens,
I want to be there for them,
to support them the way they deserve.

25
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:26,000
What would you say to someone
going through something similar?

26
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:48,000
I would tell them:
You will be okay.
Just as I try to stay strong for my children,
find something worth fighting for.
Mental illness is not shameful.
We are not crazy.
We are stronger than people think,
and life can get better.

27
00:06:48,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Nihal's story may be unique,
but her feelings are not.
Many people go through similar struggles in silence.
The most important lesson is that understanding helps,
talking matters,
and having someone who truly listens
can change everything.

28
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:24,000
If you or someone you know is struggling,
remember:
asking for help is not weakness.
It's the first real step forward.

29
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:39,000
"Behind the Silence" is more than stories.
It's a space where we try to understand ourselves
without judgment and without fear.
See you in the next episode.
Maybe the next story will be yours.

30
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
This was Shahed Ahmed,
from Behind the Silence.
